  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front will pass east of the forecast area early this\nmorning. This will bring an end to any scattered shower activity\nby sunrise. Mixing will commence fairly quickly as well so any\nfog and low clouds should dissipate early.\n\nA strong pressure gradient will be in place today which will aid\nin gusty NW winds. BUFKIT soundings indicate the likelihood of\n25 to 30 MPH gusts with some potential for a few higher gusts.\n\nHighs should rebound into the 70s at lower elevations downwind\nof the mountains, with 60s remaining in the mountains with\nupslope clouds expected. The rest of the forecast area should\nsee a good deal of sunshine.\n\nHigh pressure quickly moves overhead tonight which will allow\nfor efficient radiational cooling. Areas of frost are possible\nin the mountains as low temperatures fall into the lower to mid\n30s.\n\nThe high slowly moves offshore during the day on Tuesday\nallowing for a light southerly return flow during the afternoon.\nHighs should be within a couple of degrees of 70F area-wide.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure over New England will keep the area mostly dry and \nseasonable on Wednesday and early Thursday. Temperatures look \nto be in the 70s through the second half of the week. A low \npressure system looks to move eastward on Thursday, potentially \nbringing some rain across the northeast on Thursday afternoon \nand into early Friday. Models have not quite come into \nagreement yet for how much rain could be possible, but PWATs are\nforecast near 1.7\" which approaches the 99th percentile for \nthis time of year. High PWATs would suggest showers along this \nfrontal boundary could be efficient rain-producers. With this in\nmind, it does appear that at least wetting rains are likely, \nbut potentially more meaningful rains are possible as well.",
